1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323847 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit at $x=3$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2777af2b-a5d2-5c98-9043-4dca19a2df59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323847 #edb/ref 1074 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323863 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C={♣,♠,♡,♢,△,♣}$, then $|C|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d949443f-9b1e-55e7-9de3-ec4a5ebd3a6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323863 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $C$ is the number of elements in $C.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $C:$\n\n$$\n♣,\\,♠,\\,♡,\\,♢,\\,△\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $C$ has $5$ elements, its cardinality is $|C|=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323897 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={3,6,9,12,15,18,21,…}?$\n\n- $B={3n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4}$\n- $C={6n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 3}$\n- $D={2n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323897 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c42f9a6a-c252-590f-89ec-76e86350914b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323897 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $B,C$ and $D.$\n\n- We find the elements of the set $B$ substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3,4}$ into the formula $3n,$ and we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,3(1)=3 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,3(2)=6 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,3(3)=9 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,3(4)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $B={3,6,9,12}.$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $C$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3}$ into the formula $6n,$ and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,6(1)=6 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,6(2)=12 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,6(3)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C={6,12,18}.$\n\n- Finally, the elements of the set $D$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3,4}$ into the formula $2n,$ and we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,2(1)=2 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,2(2)=4 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,2(3)=6 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,2(4)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $D={2,4,6,8}.$\n\nNow, a set is a subset of $A$ if all of its elements are also elements of $A.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $B={3,6,9,12}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $C={6,12,18}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $D={2,4,6,8}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324064 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a78286a-924a-5b53-b1ff-20b6e774f358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324432 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324432 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38c11f6c-ec31-5baf-88b1-d11c59ff1a60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324547 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4726d096-c73d-5a7c-a29b-ed3c56e58ae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324689 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "563239d5-e805-5360-a191-da957f460fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326117 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3aeaf56-06ad-5d25-bdf7-6801ed375e59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326553 #edb/ref 10